<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/intermediate/renaissance-and-baroque">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Renaissance and Baroque are the <strong>two founding intensities of Western art</strong> —
    the measured and the dramatic — and their sequence teaches the art-history method: every
    style answers its patrons, its theology and its physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="measurement, equilibrium, drama, the Dutch market" widget="equationStep" config={renbCfg} />

  <h2>Interpretive tools for the period</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Iconography (Panofsky):</strong> symbols as a language — the lily means purity, the skull means vanity, the dog means fidelity (in the Arnolfini portrait, where everything means something): decoding the period\'s visual vocabulary is the close reading lesson with paint.</li>
    <li><strong>Patronage reads the art:</strong> Medicis (civic piety + banking guilt), Popes (ambition in marble), Dutch burghers (domestic virtue): the sociology lesson\'s class lens, curatorial edition — who paid shapes what was made.</li>
    <li><strong>Women artists, recovered:</strong> Artemisia Gentileschi\'s Judith (vengeance with a biography), Sofonisba Anguissola\'s court career — the canon lesson\'s revision in action: art history writing back the artists the older story skipped.</li>
    <li><strong>Caravaggio as case study:</strong> naturalism plus violence plus biography (the police record is real) — the intentional-fallacy debate (the theory lesson) in one extravagant life: how much does the artist\'s story explain the pictures?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Panofsky, Studies in Iconology; Gombrich; the art-history ladder\'s walk-through lesson.</p>
</TopicPage>
